WEBVTT

00:00:00.026 --> 00:00:04.800
SIEM platforms collect data from many sources, including operating systems, firewalls,

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.120
intrusion detection systems, cloud services, authentication providers, applications,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.475
EDR platforms, and network devices.

00:00:11.475 --> 00:00:15.150
This data arrives in many different formats, with different field names, timestamps,

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.590
and levels of detail.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.132
Because source systems do not all speak the same language,

00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:23.475
SIEM solutions often normalize the data into a more consistent structure.

00:00:23.475 --> 00:00:26.845
Normalization makes information from different systems easier to search, compare,

00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:28.236
and analyze together.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:31.880
Correlation rules examine events for patterns that may indicate suspicious activity.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.570
For example,

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:37.010
multiple failed logins followed by a successful login from an unusual location might

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:37.725
trigger an alert.

00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:41.940
SIEM transforms scattered logs into actionable intelligence by connecting related events

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.280
into a more useful signal.
